summary.text:
We need to write CODESYS ST FB_Task. Need timer semantics: dt_ms elapsed since preceding invocation; first invocation starts IEC timers at elapsed zero. Need start delay 30s using TON or manual accumulation? Using TON with PT:=T#30S and call each scan; TON's timing based on system clock? In simulation compile only, but tests likely run with a harness. The spec says dt_ms actual elapsed time. In CODESYS TON uses internal clock, not dt_ms input. But we have no dt_ms input in interface! Only OCCUPANCY_DETECTED, ENERGY_SAVING_MODE, HVAC_MODE_SELECTOR. So timer must use TON with real elapsed time. In CODESYS, TON instance called each cycle uses system time. Tests with dt_ms likely simulate actual time between calls. First invocation starts IEC timers at elapsed zero — TON behavior: on first call with IN TRUE, it starts and ET=0; at exactly 30s after, Q true. Let's design:

VAR
  tonDelay : TON;
  ...
END_VAR

TON(IN := permit, PT := T#30S);
SUPPLY_FAN_RUN := tonDelay.Q;

Immediately stop fan and reset timing if occupancy lost or Night: TON with IN FALSE resets ET to 0 immediately? CODESYS TON: when IN goes FALSE, ET resets to 0. Yes. When permit becomes true again, starts from 0.

But note T3 step: t=0 call, t=29.9s call, t=30.0 call expected fan true. TON Q true when ET >= PT at call; at 30s, yes.

T1 first step dt 0, etc. No issue.

Setpoint: if mode=3 -> 4. else if occupancy -> 0. else case mode: 0 -> if energy saving 3 else 2; 1 ->1; 2->4. Bound clamp. Selector valid 0..3 but could clamp anyway. Default 0? Maybe CASE.

Initialize outputs OFF/zero at power-up; outputs declared no initial values? We can initialize variables? Outputs declared in VAR_OUTPUT with initial false/0 perhaps. Requirements: "Initialize outputs to OFF and zero at power-up; update them from current inputs when normal calculation begins." Since FB outputs default FALSE/0 when instance created. We can just assign each call. But maybe initialization should only happen at first call? They mention first call calculates adjustment immediately. Fine.

Potential issue: TON with PT T#30S, calling in every cycle. The wrapper may control time? In actual CODESYS simulation, time advances by real time, not dt_ms. However test harness likely uses a virtual clock or executes with waits. The task's compile-only environment. Just write standard.

But caution: "dt_ms is elapsed time since the preceding invocation; the first invocation starts IEC timers at elapsed zero. CycleTime inputs, where supplied, are attributed to the current invocation rather than inferred from dt_ms." This suggests expected implementation uses standard IEC timers and tests run real-time-ish. But if harness calls rapidly in a loop with manipulated clock? CODESYS doesn't expose dt_ms. There is no input. So TON is right.

Need declaration: FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task followed by VAR sections; omit END_FUNCTION_BLOCK. Include VAR_INPUT, VAR_OUTPUT, VAR internal TON.

Let's draft.

declaration:
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task
VAR_INPUT
  OCCUPANCY_DETECTED : BOOL;
  ENERGY_SAVING_MODE : BOOL;
  HVAC_MODE_SELECTOR : INT;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
  SUPPLY_FAN_RUN : BOOL := FALSE;
  TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST : INT := 0;
END_VAR
VAR
  fbRunDelay : TON;
  xPermit : BOOL;
END_VAR

implementation:
xPermit := OCCUPANCY_DETECTED AND (HVAC_MODE_SELECTOR <> 3);

fbRunDelay(IN := xPermit, PT := T#30S);
SUPPLY_FAN_RUN := fbRunDelay.Q;

CASE HVAC_MODE_SELECTOR OF
  3:
    TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 4;
  1:
    IF OCCUPANCY_DETECTED THEN
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 0;
    ELSE
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 1;
    END_IF
  2:
    IF OCCUPANCY_DETECTED THEN
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 0;
    ELSE
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 4;
    END_IF
  0:
    IF OCCUPANCY_DETECTED THEN
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 0;
    ELSIF ENERGY_SAVING_MODE THEN
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 3;
    ELSE
      TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 2;
    END_IF
  ELSE
    TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := 0;
END_CASE

Clamp:
IF TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST > 4 THEN ... not necessary but P2. Could use LIMIT( -4, TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST, 4). CODESYS LIMIT function args (Mn, in, Mx). Use TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST := LIMIT(-4, TEMP_SETPOINT_ADJUST, 4); Might compile. Or manual. Keep robust manual IF.

Ordering: night immediate stop. TON with IN false resets. In same scan, Q false. Good.

Potential subtlety: If permit true continuously, at t=30s TON Q true. Good.

But requirement "Start a 30-second delay only when occupancy is detected and Night mode is not selected." Yes.

"Initialize outputs to OFF and zero at power-up; update them from current inputs when normal calculation begins." declaration initialization satisfies.

Do we need supporting_objects? None.

Let's compile.

